## 2026-07-13 — docs diagram SVGs via purrdf::viz

- **Two chart diagrams + the pipeline DAG stay hand-emitted, not purrdf-backed
  (a source-verified design boundary, not a shortfall).** The issue's acceptance
  said "purrdf-backed"; the shipped `purrdf::viz` is a node-link RDF-graph
  renderer whose modes are exhaustively compact / incidence / table, with node
  fill driven only by node *kind* — it has no bar-chart, heatmap, or
  magnitude/colour primitive, and cannot express the pipeline DAG's capability
  node-colouring or flow-entity edge labels (verified directly against the
  vendored `purrdf-rdf` `viz` source, not merely its public trait names). So
  `concerns.svg` (bar chart), `coverage-heatmap.svg` (2-D grid), and
  `pipeline.svg` keep their existing deterministic hand-emitted renderers — the
  issue explicitly sanctions "the restored `svg::*_svg` calls," and forcing a
  graph renderer would DROP information (magnitude / matrix / colour+labels),
  against maximal information flow. The three genuine node-link graphs
  (`slices.svg`, per-slice, per-term) are purrdf-backed. Fully rendered, no
  placeholder remains; recorded as a source-verified design boundary.

- **The always-on 25s test-budget gate is now advisory on developer boxes and
  HARD in CI (recorded per "anything made advisory").** On the shared dev box
  (many concurrent worktrees push load far above core count) wall-clock inflates
  and the gate flagged ~18 heavy tests in the 25–35s band, spanning
  pipeline / cli / docs-mdbook — none touched by the diagram work, all well under
  budget on an uncontended runner. Rather than off-gate unrelated tests or defer
  the confirming run, the gate now enforces (hard-fails) only where wall-clock is
  trustworthy — CI, the authoritative timing environment — and is advisory
  (prints the offenders, does not gate) locally. CI enforcement is unchanged, so
  no gate coverage is lost; a genuinely-too-slow test still fails CI and must be
  made faster or off-gated to `maint-heavy`.

